statement.p:
Define
\mathbf r(t)= \begin{cases} (0,0), & t<0,\\ (1,0), & t\ge 0. \end{cases}
This rule gives a point for every t, but it does not describe a continuous motion. At t=0, the point jumps from (0,0) to (1,0).

