subsection.p:
For computation in \mathbb R^3, there is a useful formula involving the cross
product:
\boxed{\kappa(t)=\frac{\|\mathbf r'(t)\times \mathbf r''(t)\|}{\|\mathbf r'(t)\|^3}.}

Assume the curve is C^2, regular, and has positive curvature. Write the velocity as \mathbf r'(t)=\sigma(t)\mathbf T(t), where \sigma(t)=\|\mathbf r'(t)\| is speed. Differentiate:
\mathbf a(t) \amp= \mathbf r''(t) \amp= \sigma'(t)\mathbf T(t)+\sigma(t)\mathbf T'(t).
Since \frac{d\mathbf T}{ds}=\kappa\mathbf N and \frac{ds}{dt}=\sigma, we get \mathbf T'(t)=\frac{d\mathbf T}{ds}\frac{ds}{dt}=\kappa\mathbf N\,\sigma. Therefore \boxed{\mathbf a(t)=\sigma'(t)\mathbf T(t)+\kappa(t)\sigma(t)^2\mathbf N(t).}

statement.p:
For a C^2 regular curve with positive curvature and speed
\sigma(t)=\|\mathbf r'(t)\|,
the tangential component of acceleration is
\boxed{a_T=\sigma'(t).}
The normal component of acceleration is
\boxed{a_N=\kappa(t)\sigma(t)^2.}
Thus
\boxed{\mathbf a=a_T\mathbf T+a_N\mathbf N.}

The ordered triple
\boxed{\bigl(\mathbf T(s),\mathbf N(s),\mathbf B(s)\bigr)}
is called the Frenet frame of the curve.

