subsection.p:
The symbols dx,\qquad dy are the coordinate-reading linear functions: dx(\Delta x,\Delta y)=\Delta x, and dy(\Delta x,\Delta y)=\Delta y. So if f=f(x,y), then
df_{(a,b)}(\Delta x,\Delta y) \amp= f_x(a,b)\Delta x+f_y(a,b)\Delta y \amp= f_x(a,b)\,dx(\Delta x,\Delta y) + f_y(a,b)\,dy(\Delta x,\Delta y).
This is why we write \boxed{ df=f_x\,dx+f_y\,dy. } At a specific point, \boxed{ df_{(a,b)} = f_x(a,b)\,dx+f_y(a,b)\,dy. }

