subsection.p:
In differential-form language, the same idea is even shorter. Allowed tangent vectors \mathbf v satisfy dg(\mathbf v)=0. At a constrained extremum, df(\mathbf v)=0 for every allowed tangent vector. Thus the kernel of dg is contained in the kernel of df. If dg
e0, choose a vector \mathbf w with dg(\mathbf w)=1. For any \mathbf v, the vector \mathbf v-dg(\mathbf v)\mathbf w is in that kernel, so df(\mathbf v)=df(\mathbf w)dg(\mathbf v). Setting \lambda=df(\mathbf w) proves  oxed{df=\lambda\,dg.} In coordinates, this is  oxed{
abla f=\lambda
abla g.}

statement.p:
Then there is a number \lambda such that
\boxed{\nabla f(\mathbf p)=\lambda\nabla g(\mathbf p).}
Equivalently,
\boxed{df_{\mathbf p}=\lambda\,dg_{\mathbf p}.}

subsection.p:
At a constrained extremum, 
abla f must also be perpendicular to the allowed tangent direction. Therefore 
abla f must lie in the plane spanned by the two constraint gradients:  oxed{
abla f=\lambda
abla g+\mu
abla h.}

statement.p:
Then there are numbers \lambda and \mu such that
\boxed{\nabla f(\mathbf p)=\lambda\nabla g(\mathbf p)+\mu\nabla h(\mathbf p).}
Equivalently,
\boxed{df_{\mathbf p}=\lambda\,dg_{\mathbf p}+\mu\,dh_{\mathbf p}.}

subsection.p:
Define
F(x,y)=\begin{cases}\dfrac{xy(x^2-y^2)}{x^2+y^2}, \amp (x,y)\ne(0,0),\\ 0, \amp (x,y)=(0,0).\end{cases}
At the origin, compute the mixed partials by looking along the axes.

First, for y\ne0,

  F_x(0,y) \amp= \lim_{h\to0}\frac{F(h,y)-F(0,y)}{h}
  \amp= \lim_{h\to0}\frac{hy(h^2-y^2)}{h(h^2+y^2)}
  \amp= \lim_{h\to0}\frac{y(h^2-y^2)}{h^2+y^2}
  \amp= -y.

Also F_x(0,0)=0, so near the origin along the y-axis,
F_x(0,y)=-y.
Therefore
F_{xy}(0,0)=\lim_{k\to0}\frac{F_x(0,k)-F_x(0,0)}{k}=\lim_{k\to0}\frac{-k}{k}=-1.

Now reverse the order. For x\ne0,

  F_y(x,0) \amp= \lim_{k\to0}\frac{F(x,k)-F(x,0)}{k}
  \amp= \lim_{k\to0}\frac{xk(x^2-k^2)}{k(x^2+k^2)}
  \amp= \lim_{k\to0}\frac{x(x^2-k^2)}{x^2+k^2}
  \amp= x.

Also F_y(0,0)=0, so near the origin along the x-axis,
F_y(x,0)=x.
Therefore
F_{yx}(0,0)=\lim_{h\to0}\frac{F_y(h,0)-F_y(0,0)}{h}=\lim_{h\to0}\frac{h}{h}=1.

