subsection.p:
      The main tests can be kept in one table.

\begin{array}{c|c|c}
\text{Situation} \amp \text{Candidate condition} \amp \text{What to do next} \\ \hline
\text{free interior point}
\amp
\nabla f=\mathbf 0
\amp
\text{use Hessian if second derivatives behave well}
\\[2mm]
\text{derivative fails}
\amp
\text{point is still a candidate}
\amp
\text{check directly}
\\[2mm]
\text{boundary point}
\amp
\text{ordinary }\nabla f=\mathbf 0\text{ may fail}
\amp
\text{optimize on the boundary}
\\[2mm]
\text{smooth constraint }g=c
\amp
\nabla f=\lambda\nabla g
\amp
\text{compare constrained candidates}
\\[2mm]
\text{two constraints}
\amp
\nabla f=\lambda\nabla g+\mu\nabla h
\amp
\text{compare constrained candidates}
\end{array}

Therefore
\boxed{\text{absolute minimum }-6\text{ at }(2,1),}
and
\boxed{\text{absolute maximum }0\text{ at }(0,0)\text{ and }(0,2).}

statement.p:
Let
F(x,y)= \begin{cases} \dfrac{xy(x^2-y^2)}{x^2+y^2}, \amp (x,y)\ne(0,0),\\[3mm] 0, \amp (x,y)=(0,0). \end{cases}
Compute F_{xy}(0,0) \qquad\text{and}\qquad F_{yx}(0,0). What hypothesis of the mixed-partials theorem fails?

subsubsection.p:
For the four data points, compute
\sum x_i=6, \qquad \sum x_i^2=14, \qquad \sum y_i=9, \qquad \sum x_iy_i=18.
Show that the equations become
\boxed{14m+6b=18,}
and
\boxed{6m+4b=9.}
Solve them to get
\boxed{m=\frac{9}{10}, \qquad b=\frac{9}{10}.}
So the least-squares line is
\boxed{y=\frac{9}{10}x+\frac{9}{10}.}

The Lagrange equations identify candidates, but do not yet prove a global minimum. For any positive x,y, set a=\sqrt{xy}. The inequality (\sqrt{x}-\sqrt{y})^2\ge0 gives x+y\ge2a. Keeping the base area xy=a^2 and height z=32/a^2 fixed, we therefore have A=xy+2z(x+y)\ge a^2+4az=a^2+\frac{128}{a}, with equality precisely when x=y=a. Thus minimizing the square-base family really does minimize over all positive dimensions. Write x=y=a, \qquad z=\frac{32}{a^2}. Then
\begin{aligned} A(a) \amp= a^2+2a\left(\frac{32}{a^2}\right) +2a\left(\frac{32}{a^2}\right)\\ \amp= a^2+\frac{128}{a}. \end{aligned}
Differentiate: A'(a)=2a-\frac{128}{a^2}. Set A'(a)=0: 2a=\frac{128}{a^2}. Thus a^3=64, so a=4. Also, A''(a)=2+\frac{256}{a^3}\gt 0. So this point gives a minimum along the square-base family.

