subsection.p:
The region is 0\le\theta\le2\pi, \qquad 0\le\phi\le\frac{\pi}{3}, \qquad 0\le\rho\le2. The density must integrate to 1:
1 \amp= \int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^{\pi/3}\int_0^2 C\rho\cdot \rho^2\sin\phi\,d\rho\,d\phi\,d\theta \amp= C \left(\int_0^{2\pi}d\theta\right) \left(\int_0^{\pi/3}\sin\phi\,d\phi\right) \left(\int_0^2\rho^3\,d\rho\right) \amp= C(2\pi)\left(1-\frac12\right)(4) \amp= 4\pi C.
Therefore \boxed{ C=\frac{1}{4\pi}. }

Now restrict to 0\le\rho\le1. Then
\operatorname{Prob}(\rho\le1) \amp= \int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^{\pi/3}\int_0^1 \frac{1}{4\pi}\rho^3\sin\phi\,d\rho\,d\phi\,d\theta \amp= \frac{1}{4\pi}(2\pi)\left(\frac12\right)\left(\frac14\right) \amp= \frac{1}{16}.
So \boxed{ \operatorname{Prob}(\rho\le1)=\frac{1}{16}. }

