subsection.p:
Now write a general linear change of variables as

  \begin{pmatrix}
  x\\y
  \end{pmatrix}
  =
  A
  \begin{pmatrix}
  u\\v
  \end{pmatrix}
  +
  \mathbf p_0,

where

  A=
  \begin{pmatrix}
      a \amp b\\
      c \amp d
  \end{pmatrix}

and \mathbf p_0 is a fixed translation.

In form language, this sign is automatic. As in the preceding section, dx and dy in this calculation mean the scalar differentials of the substituted coordinate functions on the uv-plane. If x=au+bv, \qquad y=cu+dv, then dx=a\,du+b\,dv, and dy=c\,du+d\,dv. Therefore
dx\wedge dy \amp= (a\,du+b\,dv)\wedge(c\,du+d\,dv) \amp= (ad-bc)\,du\wedge dv \amp= (\det A)\,du\wedge dv.
So \boxed{dx\wedge dy=(\det A)\,du\wedge dv.}

