subsection.p:
So
T^*(dx\wedge dy) \amp= (x_u\,du+x_v\,dv)\wedge(y_u\,du+y_v\,dv) \amp= x_u y_v\,du\wedge dv+x_v y_u\,dv\wedge du \amp= (x_u y_v-x_v y_u)\,du\wedge dv.
Hence \boxed{ T^*(dx\wedge dy) = \frac{\partial(x,y)}{\partial(u,v)}\,du\wedge dv. }

statement.p:
The area form pulls back as T^*(dx\wedge dy)=r\,dr\wedge d\theta. The density becomes g(r\cos\theta,r\sin\theta)=3+r\cos\theta. So the pulled-back 2-form is (3+r\cos\theta)\,r\,dr\wedge d\theta. The total amount of chemical is
\iint_R (3+x)\,dA \amp= \int_0^{\pi/2}\int_0^2 (3+r\cos\theta)r\,dr\,d\theta \amp= \int_0^{\pi/2} \left[ \frac{3r^2}{2}+\frac{r^3}{3}\cos\theta \right]_{0}^{2} d\theta \amp= \int_0^{\pi/2} \left( 6+\frac83\cos\theta \right)d\theta \amp= 3\pi+\frac83.
So \boxed{ \iint_R (3+x)\,dA=3\pi+\frac83. }

Let T(u,v,w)=(x,y,z)=(u+v,\;u-v,\;w). Then T^*(dx)=du+dv, T^*(dy)=du-dv, and T^*(dz)=dw. Therefore
T^*(dx\wedge dy\wedge dz) \amp= (du+dv)\wedge(du-dv)\wedge dw \amp= (-2\,du\wedge dv)\wedge dw \amp= -2\,du\wedge dv\wedge dw.
So \boxed{ T^*(dx\wedge dy\wedge dz) = -2\,du\wedge dv\wedge dw. }

