subsection.p:
There are three possibilities:
\begin{array}{c|c}
\mathbf F\cdot\widehat{\mathbf n}\gt0
\amp
\text{flow crosses in the positive normal direction}
\\[1mm]
\mathbf F\cdot\widehat{\mathbf n}\lt0
\amp
\text{flow crosses against the positive normal direction}
\\[1mm]
\mathbf F\cdot\widehat{\mathbf n}=0
\amp
\text{flow is tangent to the surface}
\end{array}

The dot product is \mathbf F\cdot(\mathbf r_u\times\mathbf r_v)=(2,1,5)\cdot\left(-\frac12,0,1\right)=-1+5=4. Therefore
\iint_S \mathbf F\cdot\widehat{\mathbf n}\,dS \amp= \int_0^2\int_0^3 4\,dv\,du \amp= 24.
So the upward flux through the skylight is \boxed{24.}

subsection.md:
\begin{array}{c|c|c}
\text{positive normal direction} \amp \text{ordered tangent directions} \amp \text{oriented area form}\\ \hline
\mathbf i \amp \mathbf j,\mathbf k \amp dy\wedge dz\\[1mm]
\mathbf j \amp \mathbf k,\mathbf i \amp dz\wedge dx\\[1mm]
\mathbf k \amp \mathbf i,\mathbf j \amp dx\wedge dy
\end{array}

\begin{array}{c|c}
\text{field component} \amp \text{oriented coordinate area it crosses} \\ \hline
P \amp dy\wedge dz\\[1mm]
Q \amp dz\wedge dx\\[1mm]
R \amp dx\wedge dy
\end{array}

subsection.p:
For a flux form,

  \boxed{
  \iint_S \Phi_{\mathbf F}
  =
  \iint_S \mathbf F\cdot\widehat{\mathbf n}\,dS.
  }

The two notations describe the same signed surface integral.

It says that flux is built from three signed shadows:
\boxed{ \Phi_{\mathbf F} = P\,dy\wedge dz + Q\,dz\wedge dx + R\,dx\wedge dy. }
The P-component crosses yz-area, the Q-component crosses zx-area, and the R-component crosses xy-area.

subsection.md:
\begin{array}{c|c|c}
\text{oriented coordinate area} \amp \text{positive normal} \amp \text{circulation density}\\ \hline
dy\wedge dz \amp \mathbf i \amp R_y-Q_z\\[1mm]
dz\wedge dx \amp \mathbf j \amp P_z-R_x\\[1mm]
dx\wedge dy \amp \mathbf k \amp Q_x-P_y
\end{array}

subsection.p:
For this space special case, define d\omega to have the three curl coefficients displayed below, just as the plane operation was defined in . The first line is a way to verify that coordinate definition using scalar differentials and the alternating algebra of Chapter 2; it does not assume a general product rule for exterior derivatives. Thus:
d(P\,dx+Q\,dy+R\,dz) \amp= dP\wedge dx+dQ\wedge dy+dR\wedge dz \amp= (R_y-Q_z)\,dy\wedge dz \amp\quad+ (P_z-R_x)\,dz\wedge dx \amp\quad+ (Q_x-P_y)\,dx\wedge dy.
So
\boxed{ d\omega = \Phi_{\nabla\times\mathbf F}. }
In words:
\boxed{ \text{the exterior derivative of the work form is the flux form of the curl.} }

In classical vector notation, the same statement is

  \boxed{
  \oint_C \mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r
  =
  \iint_D (\nabla\times\mathbf F)\cdot\widehat{\mathbf n}\,dS.
  }

