subsection.p:
Let \mathbf F=(P,Q,R) be a vector field in space. Its flux 2-form is \boxed{\Phi_{\mathbf F} = P\,dy\wedge dz + Q\,dz\wedge dx + R\,dx\wedge dy.} Taking the exterior derivative gives
d\Phi_{\mathbf F} \amp= dP\wedge dy\wedge dz + dQ\wedge dz\wedge dx + dR\wedge dx\wedge dy \amp= P_x\,dx\wedge dy\wedge dz \amp\quad+ Q_y\,dy\wedge dz\wedge dx \amp\quad+ R_z\,dz\wedge dx\wedge dy \amp= (P_x+Q_y+R_z)\,dx\wedge dy\wedge dz.
The coefficient is the divergence: \boxed{\nabla\cdot\mathbf F=P_x+Q_y+R_z.} So \boxed{d\Phi_{\mathbf F} = (\nabla\cdot\mathbf F)\,dx\wedge dy\wedge dz.}

Let E be the ball of radius a centered at the origin: x^2+y^2+z^2\le a^2. Let \mathbf F=(x,y,z), with flux form \Phi=x\,dy\wedge dz+y\,dz\wedge dx+z\,dx\wedge dy. As above, d\Phi=3\,dx\wedge dy\wedge dz. Therefore
\int_{\partial E}\Phi \amp= \int_E d\Phi \amp= \int_E 3\,dx\wedge dy\wedge dz \amp= 3\operatorname{Volume}(E) \amp= 3\left(\frac43\pi a^3\right) \amp= 4\pi a^3.
So \boxed{\iint_{\partial E}(x,y,z)\cdot\widehat{\mathbf n}_{\mathrm{out}}\,dS = 4\pi a^3.}

paragraphs.p:
Now integrate over the sphere S_a of radius a, oriented outward. On the sphere, \widehat{\mathbf n}_{\mathrm{out}}=\frac{(x,y,z)}{a}, and \mathbf G=\frac{(x,y,z)}{a^3}. Thus
\mathbf G\cdot\widehat{\mathbf n}_{\mathrm{out}} \amp= \frac{(x,y,z)}{a^3}\cdot\frac{(x,y,z)}{a} \amp= \frac{x^2+y^2+z^2}{a^4} \amp= \frac1{a^2}.
Therefore \boxed{\int_{S_a}\Psi = \iint_{S_a}\mathbf G\cdot\widehat{\mathbf n}_{\mathrm{out}}\,dS = \frac1{a^2}(4\pi a^2) = 4\pi.}

subsection.p:
This is the geometric meaning of
\boxed{\partial(\partial E)=\emptyset.}
A boundary has no boundary.

In vector language, this says:
\boxed{\iint_{\partial E}(\nabla\times\mathbf F)\cdot\widehat{\mathbf n}_{\mathrm{out}}\,dS=0,}
as long as \mathbf F is defined on a region containing E.

Now the single-variable Fundamental Theorem of Calculus enters twice: Q(b,y)-Q(a,y)=\int_a^b Q_x(x,y)\,dx, and P(x,d)-P(x,c)=\int_c^d P_y(x,y)\,dy. Therefore
\int_{\partial R}\omega \amp= \int_c^d\int_a^b Q_x(x,y)\,dx\,dy - \int_a^b\int_c^d P_y(x,y)\,dy\,dx \amp= \iint_R (Q_x-P_y)\,dx\wedge dy.
But d\omega=(Q_x-P_y)\,dx\wedge dy. So \boxed{\int_{\partial R}\omega=\int_R d\omega.}

The proof idea is simple enough to see:
\text{prove on simple pieces, then cancel interior boundaries.}
A fully rigorous proof has to check several details.

 proved the algebraic fact
\boxed{d^2=0.}
This chapter keeps using the geometric fact
\boxed{\partial(\partial M)=\emptyset.}

So the two slogans match:
\boxed{\text{the derivative of a derivative is zero}}
and
\boxed{\text{the boundary of a boundary is empty.}}

section.p:
    The theorem has only three moving parts:
    M,\qquad \partial M,\qquad \omega.
    Once those are chosen, everything else is forced. The degree of \omega must match the
    dimension of the boundary. The degree of d\omega then matches the dimension of the
    inside.

\boxed{
\begin{array}{c}
\omega \text{ is integrated over } \partial M,\\[1mm]
d\omega \text{ is integrated over } M.
\end{array}
}

subsection.p:
A vector field
\mathbf F=(P,Q,R)
has a work 1-form
\boxed{\omega_{\mathbf F}=P\,dx+Q\,dy+R\,dz.}
Its exterior derivative is the flux 2-form of the curl:
\boxed{d\omega_{\mathbf F}=\Phi_{\nabla\times\mathbf F}.}

The same vector field also has a flux 2-form
\boxed{\Phi_{\mathbf F}=P\,dy\wedge dz+Q\,dz\wedge dx+R\,dx\wedge dy.}
Its exterior derivative is the divergence volume form:
\boxed{d\Phi_{\mathbf F}=(\nabla\cdot\mathbf F)\,dx\wedge dy\wedge dz.}

The exterior derivative is
\boxed{ \begin{aligned} d\omega &= (R_y-Q_z)\,dy\wedge dz\\ &\quad+ (P_z-R_x)\,dz\wedge dx\\ &\quad+ (Q_x-P_y)\,dx\wedge dy. \end{aligned} }
The coefficients are the components of \nabla\times\mathbf F. So d\omega=\Phi_{\nabla\times\mathbf F}.

The vector formulas look different because the geometry looks different: endpoints, curves,
surfaces, and solids. But the forms formula does not change:
\boxed{\int_{\partial M}\omega=\int_M d\omega.}

