subsection.p:
Now compute the outward flux through the six faces.
\begin{array}{c|c|c|c} \text{Face} \amp \widehat{\mathbf n} \amp \mathbf J\cdot\widehat{\mathbf n} \amp \text{Flux} \\ \hline x=1 \amp (1,0,0) \amp 1 \amp 1\\ x=0 \amp (-1,0,0) \amp 0 \amp 0\\ y=1 \amp (0,1,0) \amp 1 \amp 1\\ y=0 \amp (0,-1,0) \amp 0 \amp 0\\ z=1 \amp (0,0,1) \amp 1 \amp 1\\ z=0 \amp (0,0,-1) \amp 0 \amp 0 \end{array}
So the total outward flux is
1+0+1+0+1+0=3.
The two sides match:
\boxed{ \iint_{\partial E}\mathbf J\cdot\widehat{\mathbf n}\,dS = \iiint_E\nabla\cdot\mathbf J\,dV = 3. }

introduction.p:
The point of forms is not to make old problems look fancier. The point is to make the
shape of the theorem visible:
\boxed{\int_{\partial M}\omega=\int_M d\omega.}

subsection.p:
For work or circulation, we use a 1-form:
\boxed{\omega=P\,dx+Q\,dy}
in the plane, or
\boxed{\omega=P\,dx+Q\,dy+R\,dz}
in space.

Let C be the unit circle, oriented counterclockwise: x=\cos t,\qquad y=\sin t,\qquad 0\le t\le2\pi. Then dx=-\sin t\,dt, \qquad dy=\cos t\,dt. Pull back the form:
\omega \amp = -\sin t(-\sin t\,dt)+\cos t(\cos t\,dt) \amp = (\sin^2t+\cos^2t)\,dt \amp = dt.
Therefore \boxed{\oint_C\omega=\int_0^{2\pi}dt=2\pi.}

